---@class elasticsearch.CatMlJobsJobsRecord
---@field id? string The anomaly detection job identifier.
---@field state? elasticsearch.MlTypesJobState The status of the anomaly detection job.
---@field opened_time? string For open jobs only, the amount of time the job has been opened.
---@field assignment_explanation? string For open anomaly detection jobs only, contains messages relating to the selection of a node to run the job.
---@field data_processed_records? string The number of input documents that have been processed by the anomaly detection job. This value includes documents with missing fields, since they are nonetheless analyzed. If you use datafeeds and have aggregations in your search query, the `processed_record_count` is the number of aggregation results processed, not the number of Elasticsearch documents.
---@field data_processed_fields? string The total number of fields in all the documents that have been processed by the anomaly detection job. Only fields that are specified in the detector configuration object contribute to this count. The timestamp is not included in this count.
---@field data_input_bytes? elasticsearch.TypesByteSize The number of bytes of input data posted to the anomaly detection job.
---@field data_input_records? string The number of input documents posted to the anomaly detection job.
---@field data_input_fields? string The total number of fields in input documents posted to the anomaly detection job. This count includes fields that are not used in the analysis. However, be aware that if you are using a datafeed, it extracts only the required fields from the documents it retrieves before posting them to the job.
---@field data_invalid_dates? string The number of input documents with either a missing date field or a date that could not be parsed.
---@field data_missing_fields? string The number of input documents that are missing a field that the anomaly detection job is configured to analyze. Input documents with missing fields are still processed because it is possible that not all fields are missing. If you are using datafeeds or posting data to the job in JSON format, a high `missing_field_count` is often not an indication of data issues. It is not necessarily a cause for concern.
---@field data_out_of_order_timestamps? string The number of input documents that have a timestamp chronologically preceding the start of the current anomaly detection bucket offset by the latency window. This information is applicable only when you provide data to the anomaly detection job by using the post data API. These out of order documents are discarded, since jobs require time series data to be in ascending chronological order.
---@field data_empty_buckets? string The number of buckets which did not contain any data. If your data contains many empty buckets, consider increasing your `bucket_span` or using functions that are tolerant to gaps in data such as mean, `non_null_sum` or `non_zero_count`.
---@field data_sparse_buckets? string The number of buckets that contained few data points compared to the expected number of data points. If your data contains many sparse buckets, consider using a longer `bucket_span`.
---@field data_buckets? string The total number of buckets processed.
---@field data_earliest_record? string The timestamp of the earliest chronologically input document.
---@field data_latest_record? string The timestamp of the latest chronologically input document.
---@field data_last? string The timestamp at which data was last analyzed, according to server time.
---@field data_last_empty_bucket? string The timestamp of the last bucket that did not contain any data.
---@field data_last_sparse_bucket? string The timestamp of the last bucket that was considered sparse.
---@field model_bytes? elasticsearch.TypesByteSize The number of bytes of memory used by the models. This is the maximum value since the last time the model was persisted. If the job is closed, this value indicates the latest size.
---@field model_memory_status? elasticsearch.MlTypesMemoryStatus The status of the mathematical models.
---@field model_bytes_exceeded? elasticsearch.TypesByteSize The number of bytes over the high limit for memory usage at the last allocation failure.
---@field model_memory_limit? string The upper limit for model memory usage, checked on increasing values.
---@field model_by_fields? string The number of `by` field values that were analyzed by the models. This value is cumulative for all detectors in the job.
---@field model_over_fields? string The number of `over` field values that were analyzed by the models. This value is cumulative for all detectors in the job.
---@field model_partition_fields? string The number of `partition` field values that were analyzed by the models. This value is cumulative for all detectors in the job.
---@field model_bucket_allocation_failures? string The number of buckets for which new entities in incoming data were not processed due to insufficient model memory. This situation is also signified by a `hard_limit: memory_status` property value.
---@field model_categorization_status? elasticsearch.MlTypesCategorizationStatus The status of categorization for the job.
---@field model_categorized_doc_count? string The number of documents that have had a field categorized.
---@field model_total_category_count? string The number of categories created by categorization.
---@field model_frequent_category_count? string The number of categories that match more than 1% of categorized documents.
---@field model_rare_category_count? string The number of categories that match just one categorized document.
---@field model_dead_category_count? string The number of categories created by categorization that will never be assigned again because another category’s definition makes it a superset of the dead category. Dead categories are a side effect of the way categorization has no prior training.
---@field model_failed_category_count? string The number of times that categorization wanted to create a new category but couldn’t because the job had hit its `model_memory_limit`. This count does not track which specific categories failed to be created. Therefore you cannot use this value to determine the number of unique categories that were missed.
---@field model_log_time? string The timestamp when the model stats were gathered, according to server time.
---@field model_timestamp? string The timestamp of the last record when the model stats were gathered.
---@field forecasts_total? string The number of individual forecasts currently available for the job. A value of one or more indicates that forecasts exist.
---@field forecasts_memory_min? string The minimum memory usage in bytes for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field forecasts_memory_max? string The maximum memory usage in bytes for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field forecasts_memory_avg? string The average memory usage in bytes for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field forecasts_memory_total? string The total memory usage in bytes for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field forecasts_records_min? string The minimum number of `model_forecast` documents written for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field forecasts_records_max? string The maximum number of `model_forecast` documents written for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field forecasts_records_avg? string The average number of `model_forecast` documents written for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field forecasts_records_total? string The total number of `model_forecast` documents written for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field forecasts_time_min? string The minimum runtime in milliseconds for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field forecasts_time_max? string The maximum runtime in milliseconds for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field forecasts_time_avg? string The average runtime in milliseconds for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field forecasts_time_total? string The total runtime in milliseconds for forecasts related to the anomaly detection job.
---@field node_id? string The uniqe identifier of the assigned node.
---@field node_name? string The name of the assigned node.
---@field node_ephemeral_id? string The ephemeral identifier of the assigned node.
---@field node_address? string The network address of the assigned node.
---@field buckets_count? string The number of bucket results produced by the job.
---@field buckets_time_total? string The sum of all bucket processing times, in milliseconds.
---@field buckets_time_min? string The minimum of all bucket processing times, in milliseconds.
---@field buckets_time_max? string The maximum of all bucket processing times, in milliseconds.
---@field buckets_time_exp_avg? string The exponential moving average of all bucket processing times, in milliseconds.
