---@class elasticsearch.DeleteRequest
---@field index string The name of the target index.
---@field id string A unique identifier for the document.
---@field if_primary_term? number Only perform the operation if the document has this primary term.
---@field if_seq_no? number Only perform the operation if the document has this sequence number.
---@field refresh? elasticsearch.TypesRefresh|string If `true`, Elasticsearch refreshes the affected shards to make this operation visible to search. If `wait_for`, it waits for a refresh to make this operation visible to search. If `false`, it does nothing with refreshes.
---@field routing? elasticsearch.TypesRouting A custom value used to route operations to a specific shard. Not allowed when `index.slice.enabled` is `true` for the target index; use `_slice` instead.
---@field timeout? elasticsearch.TypesDuration The period to wait for active shards. This parameter is useful for situations where the primary shard assigned to perform the delete operation might not be available when the delete operation runs. Some reasons for this might be that the primary shard is currently recovering from a store or undergoing relocation. By default, the delete operation will wait on the primary shard to become available for up to 1 minute before failing and responding with an error.
---@field version? number An explicit version number for concurrency control. It must match the current version of the document for the request to succeed.
---@field version_type? elasticsearch.TypesVersionType|string The version type.
---@field wait_for_active_shards? elasticsearch.TypesWaitForActiveShards The minimum number of shard copies that must be active before proceeding with the operation. You can set it to `all` or any positive integer up to the total number of shards in the index (`number_of_replicas+1`). The default value of `1` means it waits for each primary shard to be active.

---@class elasticsearch.DeleteScriptRequest
---@field id string The identifier for the stored script or search template.
---@field master_timeout? elasticsearch.TypesDuration The period to wait for a connection to the master node. If no response is received before the timeout expires, the request fails and returns an error. It can also be set to `-1` to indicate that the request should never timeout.
---@field timeout? elasticsearch.TypesDuration The period to wait for a response. If no response is received before the timeout expires, the request fails and returns an error. It can also be set to `-1` to indicate that the request should never timeout.

---@class elasticsearch.DeleteSnapshotRequest
---@field repository string The name of the repository to delete a snapshot from.
---@field snapshot string A comma-separated list of snapshot names to delete. It also accepts wildcards (`*`).
---@field master_timeout? elasticsearch.TypesDuration The period to wait for the master node. If the master node is not available before the timeout expires, the request fails and returns an error. To indicate that the request should never timeout, set it to `-1`.
---@field wait_for_completion? boolean If `false`, the request returns a response as soon as the deletes are scheduled. If `true`, the request returns a response when the matching snapshots are all deleted, and the post-deletion cleanup work associated with the request has completed. If you make several requests to the delete-snapshots API targetting overlapping collections of snapshots then some of those requests may perform different parts of the associated post-deletion cleanup work, returning their responses at different times. For example, if you make two requests to delete the same snapshot then sometimes all of the post-deletion cleanup work will be associated with the first request, delaying its response, while the second request has no associated post-deletion cleanup work and receives its response as soon as the snapshot has been deleted.

