---@class elasticsearch.GlobalSearchTypesHighlight
---@field type? elasticsearch.GlobalSearchTypesHighlighterType
---@field boundary_chars? string A string that contains each boundary character.
---@field boundary_max_scan? number How far to scan for boundary characters.
---@field boundary_scanner? elasticsearch.GlobalSearchTypesBoundaryScanner|string Specifies how to break the highlighted fragments: chars, sentence, or word. Only valid for the unified and fvh highlighters. Defaults to `sentence` for the `unified` highlighter. Defaults to `chars` for the `fvh` highlighter.
---@field boundary_scanner_locale? string Controls which locale is used to search for sentence and word boundaries. This parameter takes a form of a language tag, for example: `"en-US"`, `"fr-FR"`, `"ja-JP"`.
---@field force_source? boolean
---@field fragmenter? elasticsearch.GlobalSearchTypesHighlighterFragmenter|string Specifies how text should be broken up in highlight snippets: `simple` or `span`. Only valid for the `plain` highlighter.
---@field fragment_size? number The size of the highlighted fragment in characters.
---@field highlight_filter? boolean
---@field highlight_query? elasticsearch.TypesQueryDslQueryContainer Highlight matches for a query other than the search query. This is especially useful if you use a rescore query because those are not taken into account by highlighting by default.
---@field max_fragment_length? number
---@field max_analyzed_offset? number If set to a non-negative value, highlighting stops at this defined maximum limit. The rest of the text is not processed, thus not highlighted and no error is returned The `max_analyzed_offset` query setting does not override the `index.highlight.max_analyzed_offset` setting, which prevails when it’s set to lower value than the query setting.
---@field no_match_size? number The amount of text you want to return from the beginning of the field if there are no matching fragments to highlight.
---@field number_of_fragments? number The maximum number of fragments to return. If the number of fragments is set to `0`, no fragments are returned. Instead, the entire field contents are highlighted and returned. This can be handy when you need to highlight short texts such as a title or address, but fragmentation is not required. If `number_of_fragments` is `0`, `fragment_size` is ignored.
---@field options? elasticsearch.GlobalSearchTypesHighlightOptionsMap
---@field order? elasticsearch.GlobalSearchTypesHighlighterOrder|string Sorts highlighted fragments by score when set to `score`. By default, fragments will be output in the order they appear in the field (order: `none`). Setting this option to `score` will output the most relevant fragments first. Each highlighter applies its own logic to compute relevancy scores.
---@field phrase_limit? number Controls the number of matching phrases in a document that are considered. Prevents the `fvh` highlighter from analyzing too many phrases and consuming too much memory. When using `matched_fields`, `phrase_limit` phrases per matched field are considered. Raising the limit increases query time and consumes more memory. Only supported by the `fvh` highlighter.
---@field post_tags? elasticsearch.GlobalSearchTypesHighlightPostTagsList Use in conjunction with `pre_tags` to define the HTML tags to use for the highlighted text. By default, highlighted text is wrapped in `<em>` and `</em>` tags.
---@field pre_tags? elasticsearch.GlobalSearchTypesHighlightPreTagsList Use in conjunction with `post_tags` to define the HTML tags to use for the highlighted text. By default, highlighted text is wrapped in `<em>` and `</em>` tags.
---@field require_field_match? boolean By default, only fields that contains a query match are highlighted. Set to `false` to highlight all fields.
---@field tags_schema? elasticsearch.GlobalSearchTypesHighlighterTagsSchema|string Set to `styled` to use the built-in tag schema.
---@field encoder? elasticsearch.GlobalSearchTypesHighlighterEncoder|string
---@field fields elasticsearch.GlobalSearchTypesHighlightFields

