---@field by_field_name? string
---@field by_field_value? string
---@field correlated_by_field_value? string
---@field field_name? string
---@field ["function"]? string
---@field function_description? string
---@field geo_results? elasticsearch.MlTypesGeoResults
---@field influencers? elasticsearch.MlTypesAnomalyCauseInfluencersList
---@field over_field_name? string
---@field over_field_value? string
---@field partition_field_name? string
---@field partition_field_value? string
---@field probability number
---@field typical? elasticsearch.MlTypesAnomalyCauseTypicalList

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesAnomalyCauseActualList number[]

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesAnomalyCauseInfluencersList elasticsearch.MlTypesInfluence[]

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesAnomalyCauseTypicalList number[]

---For population analysis, an over field must be specified in the detector. This property contains an array of anomaly records that are the causes for the anomaly that has been identified for the over field. This sub-resource contains the most anomalous records for the `over_field_name`. For scalability reasons, a maximum of the 10 most significant causes of the anomaly are returned. As part of the core analytical modeling, these low-level anomaly records are aggregated for their parent over field record. The `causes` resource contains similar elements to the record resource, namely `actual`, `typical`, `geo_results.actual_point`, `geo_results.typical_point`, `*_field_name` and `*_field_value`. Probability and scores are not applicable to causes.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesAnomalyCausesList elasticsearch.MlTypesAnomalyCause[]

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesAnomalyExplanation
---@field anomaly_characteristics_impact? number Impact from the duration and magnitude of the detected anomaly relative to the historical average.
---@field anomaly_length? number Length of the detected anomaly in the number of buckets.
---@field anomaly_type? string Type of the detected anomaly: `spike` or `dip`.
---@field high_variance_penalty? boolean Indicates reduction of anomaly score for the bucket with large confidence intervals. If a bucket has large confidence intervals, the score is reduced.
---@field incomplete_bucket_penalty? boolean If the bucket contains fewer samples than expected, the score is reduced.
---@field lower_confidence_bound? number Lower bound of the 95% confidence interval.
---@field multi_bucket_impact? number Impact of the deviation between actual and typical values in the past 12 buckets.
---@field single_bucket_impact? number Impact of the deviation between actual and typical values in the current bucket.
---@field typical_value? number Typical (expected) value for this bucket.
---@field upper_confidence_bound? number Upper bound of the 95% confidence interval.

---If influencers were specified in the detector configuration, this array contains influencers that contributed to or were to blame for an anomaly.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesAnomalyInfluencersList elasticsearch.MlTypesInfluence[]

---The typical value for the bucket, according to analytical modeling.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesAnomalyTypicalList number[]

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesApiKeyAuthorization
---@field id string The identifier for the API key.
---@field name string The name of the API key.

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesAppliesTo "actual"|"typical"|"diff_from_typical"|"time"

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesBucketInfluencer
---@field anomaly_score number A normalized score between 0-100, which is calculated for each bucket influencer. This score might be updated as newer data is analyzed.
---@field bucket_span number The length of the bucket in seconds. This value matches the bucket span that is specified in the job.
---@field influencer_field_name string The field name of the influencer.
---@field initial_anomaly_score number The score between 0-100 for each bucket influencer. This score is the initial value that was calculated at the time the bucket was processed.
---@field is_interim boolean If true, this is an interim result. In other words, the results are calculated based on partial input data.
---@field job_id string Identifier for the anomaly detection job.
---@field probability number The probability that the bucket has this behavior, in the range 0 to 1. This value can be held to a high precision of over 300 decimal places, so the `anomaly_score` is provided as a human-readable and friendly interpretation of this.
---@field raw_anomaly_score number Internal.
---@field result_type string Internal. This value is always set to `bucket_influencer`.
---@field timestamp number The start time of the bucket for which these results were calculated.
---@field timestamp_string? elasticsearch.TypesDateTime The start time of the bucket for which these results were calculated.

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesBucketSummary
---@field anomaly_score number The maximum anomaly score, between 0-100, for any of the bucket influencers. This is an overall, rate-limited score for the job. All the anomaly records in the bucket contribute to this score. This value might be updated as new data is analyzed.
---@field bucket_influencers elasticsearch.MlTypesBucketSummaryBucketInfluencersList
---@field bucket_span number The length of the bucket in seconds. This value matches the bucket span that is specified in the job.
---@field event_count number The number of input data records processed in this bucket.
---@field initial_anomaly_score number The maximum anomaly score for any of the bucket influencers. This is the initial value that was calculated at the time the bucket was processed.
---@field is_interim boolean If true, this is an interim result. In other words, the results are calculated based on partial input data.
---@field job_id string Identifier for the anomaly detection job.
---@field processing_time_ms number The amount of time, in milliseconds, that it took to analyze the bucket contents and calculate results.
---@field result_type string Internal. This value is always set to bucket.
---@field timestamp number The start time of the bucket. This timestamp uniquely identifies the bucket. Events that occur exactly at the timestamp of the bucket are included in the results for the bucket.
---@field timestamp_string? elasticsearch.TypesDateTime The start time of the bucket. This timestamp uniquely identifies the bucket. Events that occur exactly at the timestamp of the bucket are included in the results for the bucket.

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesBucketSummaryBucketInfluencersList elasticsearch.MlTypesBucketInfluencer[]

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesCalendarEvent
---@field calendar_id? string A string that uniquely identifies a calendar.
---@field event_id? string
---@field description string A description of the scheduled event.
---@field end_time elasticsearch.TypesDateTime The timestamp for the end of the scheduled event in milliseconds since the epoch or ISO 8601 format.
---@field start_time elasticsearch.TypesDateTime The timestamp for the beginning of the scheduled event in milliseconds since the epoch or ISO 8601 format.
---@field skip_result? boolean When true the model will not create results for this calendar period.
---@field skip_model_update? boolean When true the model will not be updated for this calendar period.
---@field force_time_shift? number Shift time by this many seconds. For example adjust time for daylight savings changes

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesCategorizationAnalyzer string|elasticsearch.MlTypesCategorizationAnalyzerDefinition

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesCategorizationAnalyzerDefinition
---@field char_filter? elasticsearch.MlTypesCategorizationAnalyzerDefinitionCharFilterList One or more character filters. In addition to the built-in character filters, other plugins can provide more character filters. If this property is not specified, no character filters are applied prior to categorization. If you are customizing some other aspect of the analyzer and you need to achieve the equivalent of `categorization_filters` (which are not permitted when some other aspect of the analyzer is customized), add them here as pattern replace character filters.
---@field filter? elasticsearch.MlTypesCategorizationAnalyzerDefinitionFilterList One or more token filters. In addition to the built-in token filters, other plugins can provide more token filters. If this property is not specified, no token filters are applied prior to categorization.
---@field tokenizer? elasticsearch.TypesAnalysisTokenizer The name or definition of the tokenizer to use after character filters are applied. This property is compulsory if `categorization_analyzer` is specified as an object. Machine learning provides a tokenizer called `ml_standard` that tokenizes in a way that has been determined to produce good categorization results on a variety of log file formats for logs in English. If you want to use that tokenizer but change the character or token filters, specify "tokenizer": "ml_standard" in your `categorization_analyzer`. Additionally, the `ml_classic` tokenizer is available, which tokenizes in the same way as the non-customizable tokenizer in old versions of the product (before 6.2). `ml_classic` was the default categorization tokenizer in versions 6.2 to 7.13, so if you need categorization identical to the default for jobs created in these versions, specify "tokenizer": "ml_classic" in your `categorization_analyzer`.

---One or more character filters. In addition to the built-in character filters, other plugins can provide more character filters. If this property is not specified, no character filters are applied prior to categorization. If you are customizing some other aspect of the analyzer and you need to achieve the equivalent of `categorization_filters` (which are not permitted when some other aspect of the analyzer is customized), add them here as pattern replace character filters.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesCategorizationAnalyzerDefinitionCharFilterList elasticsearch.TypesAnalysisCharFilter[]

---One or more token filters. In addition to the built-in token filters, other plugins can provide more token filters. If this property is not specified, no token filters are applied prior to categorization.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesCategorizationAnalyzerDefinitionFilterList elasticsearch.TypesAnalysisTokenFilter[]

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesCategorizationStatus "ok"|"warn"

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesCategory
---@field category_id number A unique identifier for the category. category_id is unique at the job level, even when per-partition categorization is enabled.
---@field examples elasticsearch.MlTypesCategoryExamplesList A list of examples of actual values that matched the category.
---@field grok_pattern? string [experimental] A Grok pattern that could be used in Logstash or an ingest pipeline to extract fields from messages that match the category. This field is experimental and may be changed or removed in a future release. The Grok patterns that are found are not optimal, but are often a good starting point for manual tweaking.
---@field job_id string Identifier for the anomaly detection job.
---@field max_matching_length number The maximum length of the fields that matched the category. The value is increased by 10% to enable matching for similar fields that have not been analyzed.
---@field partition_field_name? string If per-partition categorization is enabled, this property identifies the field used to segment the categorization. It is not present when per-partition categorization is disabled.
---@field partition_field_value? string If per-partition categorization is enabled, this property identifies the value of the partition_field_name for the category. It is not present when per-partition categorization is disabled.
---@field regex string A regular expression that is used to search for values that match the category.
---@field terms string A space separated list of the common tokens that are matched in values of the category.
---@field num_matches? number The number of messages that have been matched by this category. This is only guaranteed to have the latest accurate count after a job _flush or _close
---@field preferred_to_categories? elasticsearch.MlTypesCategoryPreferredToCategoriesList A list of category_id entries that this current category encompasses. Any new message that is processed by the categorizer will match against this category and not any of the categories in this list. This is only guaranteed to have the latest accurate list of categories after a job _flush or _close
---@field p? string
---@field result_type string
---@field mlcategory string

---A list of examples of actual values that matched the category.
