---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDataDescription
---@field format? string Only JSON format is supported at this time.
---@field time_field? string The name of the field that contains the timestamp.
---@field time_format? string The time format, which can be `epoch`, `epoch_ms`, or a custom pattern. The value `epoch` refers to UNIX or Epoch time (the number of seconds since 1 Jan 1970). The value `epoch_ms` indicates that time is measured in milliseconds since the epoch. The `epoch` and `epoch_ms` time formats accept either integer or real values. Custom patterns must conform to the Java DateTimeFormatter class. When you use date-time formatting patterns, it is recommended that you provide the full date, time and time zone. For example: `yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ssX`. If the pattern that you specify is not sufficient to produce a complete timestamp, job creation fails.
---@field field_delimiter? string

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeed
---@field aggregations? elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedAggregationsMap
---@field authorization? elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedAuthorization The security privileges that the datafeed uses to run its queries. If Elastic Stack security features were disabled at the time of the most recent update to the datafeed, this property is omitted.
---@field chunking_config? elasticsearch.MlTypesChunkingConfig
---@field datafeed_id string
---@field frequency? elasticsearch.TypesDuration The interval at which scheduled queries are made while the datafeed runs in real time. The default value is either the bucket span for short bucket spans, or, for longer bucket spans, a sensible fraction of the bucket span. For example: `150s`. When `frequency` is shorter than the bucket span, interim results for the last (partial) bucket are written then eventually overwritten by the full bucket results. If the datafeed uses aggregations, this value must be divisible by the interval of the date histogram aggregation.
---@field indices elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedIndicesList
---@field indexes? elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedIndexesList
---@field job_id string
---@field max_empty_searches? number
---@field query elasticsearch.TypesQueryDslQueryContainer
---@field query_delay? elasticsearch.TypesDuration
---@field script_fields? elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedScriptFieldsMap
---@field scroll_size? number
---@field delayed_data_check_config elasticsearch.MlTypesDelayedDataCheckConfig
---@field runtime_mappings? elasticsearch.TypesMappingRuntimeFields
---@field indices_options? elasticsearch.TypesIndicesOptions

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedAggregationsMap { [string]: elasticsearch.TypesAggregationsAggregationContainer }

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedAuthorization
---@field api_key? elasticsearch.MlTypesApiKeyAuthorization If an API key was used for the most recent update to the datafeed, its name and identifier are listed in the response.
---@field roles? elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedAuthorizationRolesList If a user ID was used for the most recent update to the datafeed, its roles at the time of the update are listed in the response.
---@field service_account? string If a service account was used for the most recent update to the datafeed, the account name is listed in the response.

---If a user ID was used for the most recent update to the datafeed, its roles at the time of the update are listed in the response.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedAuthorizationRolesList string[]

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedConfig
---@field aggregations? elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedConfigAggregationsMap If set, the datafeed performs aggregation searches. Support for aggregations is limited and should be used only with low cardinality data.
---@field chunking_config? elasticsearch.MlTypesChunkingConfig Datafeeds might be required to search over long time periods, for several months or years. This search is split into time chunks in order to ensure the load on Elasticsearch is managed. Chunking configuration controls how the size of these time chunks are calculated and is an advanced configuration option.
---@field datafeed_id? string A numerical character string that uniquely identifies the datafeed. This identifier can contain lowercase alphanumeric characters (a-z and 0-9), hyphens, and underscores. It must start and end with alphanumeric characters. The default value is the job identifier.
---@field delayed_data_check_config? elasticsearch.MlTypesDelayedDataCheckConfig Specifies whether the datafeed checks for missing data and the size of the window. The datafeed can optionally search over indices that have already been read in an effort to determine whether any data has subsequently been added to the index. If missing data is found, it is a good indication that the `query_delay` option is set too low and the data is being indexed after the datafeed has passed that moment in time. This check runs only on real-time datafeeds.
---@field frequency? elasticsearch.TypesDuration The interval at which scheduled queries are made while the datafeed runs in real time. The default value is either the bucket span for short bucket spans, or, for longer bucket spans, a sensible fraction of the bucket span. For example: `150s`. When `frequency` is shorter than the bucket span, interim results for the last (partial) bucket are written then eventually overwritten by the full bucket results. If the datafeed uses aggregations, this value must be divisible by the interval of the date histogram aggregation.
---@field indices? elasticsearch.TypesIndices An array of index names. Wildcards are supported. If any indices are in remote clusters, the machine learning nodes must have the `remote_cluster_client` role.
---@field indices_options? elasticsearch.TypesIndicesOptions Specifies index expansion options that are used during search.
---@field job_id? string
---@field max_empty_searches? number If a real-time datafeed has never seen any data (including during any initial training period) then it will automatically stop itself and close its associated job after this many real-time searches that return no documents. In other words, it will stop after `frequency` times `max_empty_searches` of real-time operation. If not set then a datafeed with no end time that sees no data will remain started until it is explicitly stopped.
---@field max_consecutive_extraction_failures? number The maximum number of consecutive real-time data extraction failures the datafeed tolerates before it automatically stops itself, leaving the associated job open. The consecutive-failure counter resets on any cycle that extracts successfully, including empty-data cycles. If not set, the threshold defaults to roughly one day's worth of searches based on the datafeed `frequency` (floored at 1). Set to `-1` to disable auto-stop and retry indefinitely. Values of `0` or less than `-1` are rejected.
---@field query? elasticsearch.TypesQueryDslQueryContainer The Elasticsearch query domain-specific language (DSL). This value corresponds to the query object in an Elasticsearch search POST body. All the options that are supported by Elasticsearch can be used, as this object is passed verbatim to Elasticsearch.
---@field query_delay? elasticsearch.TypesDuration The number of seconds behind real time that data is queried. For example, if data from 10:04 a.m. might not be searchable in Elasticsearch until 10:06 a.m., set this property to 120 seconds. The default value is randomly selected between `60s` and `120s`. This randomness improves the query performance when there are multiple jobs running on the same node.
---@field runtime_mappings? elasticsearch.TypesMappingRuntimeFields Specifies runtime fields for the datafeed search.
---@field script_fields? elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedConfigScriptFieldsMap Specifies scripts that evaluate custom expressions and returns script fields to the datafeed. The detector configuration objects in a job can contain functions that use these script fields.
---@field scroll_size? number The size parameter that is used in Elasticsearch searches when the datafeed does not use aggregations. The maximum value is the value of `index.max_result_window`, which is 10,000 by default.

---If set, the datafeed performs aggregation searches. Support for aggregations is limited and should be used only with low cardinality data.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedConfigAggregationsMap { [string]: elasticsearch.TypesAggregationsAggregationContainer }

---Specifies scripts that evaluate custom expressions and returns script fields to the datafeed. The detector configuration objects in a job can contain functions that use these script fields.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedConfigScriptFieldsMap { [string]: elasticsearch.TypesScriptField }

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedIndexesList string[]

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedIndicesList string[]

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedRunningState
---@field real_time_configured boolean Indicates if the datafeed is "real-time"; meaning that the datafeed has no configured `end` time.
---@field real_time_running boolean Indicates whether the datafeed has finished running on the available past data. For datafeeds without a configured `end` time, this means that the datafeed is now running on "real-time" data.
---@field search_interval? elasticsearch.MlTypesRunningStateSearchInterval Provides the latest time interval the datafeed has searched.

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedScriptFieldsMap { [string]: elasticsearch.TypesScriptField }

---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedState "started"|"stopped"|"starting"|"stopping"

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedStats
---@field assignment_explanation? string For started datafeeds only, contains messages relating to the selection of a node.
---@field datafeed_id string A numerical character string that uniquely identifies the datafeed. This identifier can contain lowercase alphanumeric characters (a-z and 0-9), hyphens, and underscores. It must start and end with alphanumeric characters.
---@field node? elasticsearch.MlTypesDiscoveryNodeCompact For started datafeeds only, this information pertains to the node upon which the datafeed is started.
---@field state elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedState The status of the datafeed, which can be one of the following values: `starting`, `started`, `stopping`, `stopped`.
---@field timing_stats? elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedTimingStats An object that provides statistical information about timing aspect of this datafeed.
---@field running_state? elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedRunningState An object containing the running state for this datafeed. It is only provided if the datafeed is started.

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDatafeedTimingStats
---@field bucket_count number The number of buckets processed.
---@field exponential_average_search_time_per_hour_ms number The exponential average search time per hour, in milliseconds.
---@field exponential_average_calculation_context? elasticsearch.MlTypesExponentialAverageCalculationContext
---@field job_id string Identifier for the anomaly detection job.
---@field search_count number The number of searches run by the datafeed.
---@field total_search_time_ms number The total time the datafeed spent searching, in milliseconds.
---@field average_search_time_per_bucket_ms? number The average search time per bucket, in milliseconds.

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisAnalyzedFields
---@field includes? elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisAnalyzedFieldsIncludesList An array of strings that defines the fields that will be excluded from the analysis. You do not need to add fields with unsupported data types to excludes, these fields are excluded from the analysis automatically.
---@field excludes? elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisAnalyzedFieldsExcludesList An array of strings that defines the fields that will be included in the analysis.

---An array of strings that defines the fields that will be included in the analysis.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisAnalyzedFieldsExcludesList string[]

---An array of strings that defines the fields that will be excluded from the analysis. You do not need to add fields with unsupported data types to excludes, these fields are excluded from the analysis automatically.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisAnalyzedFieldsIncludesList string[]

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisClassification
---@field alpha? number Advanced configuration option. Machine learning uses loss guided tree growing, which means that the decision trees grow where the regularized loss decreases most quickly. This parameter affects loss calculations by acting as a multiplier of the tree depth. Higher alpha values result in shallower trees and faster training times. By default, this value is calculated during hyperparameter optimization. It must be greater than or equal to zero.
---@field dependent_variable string Defines which field of the document is to be predicted. It must match one of the fields in the index being used to train. If this field is missing from a document, then that document will not be used for training, but a prediction with the trained model will be generated for it. It is also known as continuous target variable. For classification analysis, the data type of the field must be numeric (`integer`, `short`, `long`, `byte`), categorical (`ip` or `keyword`), or `boolean`. There must be no more than 30 different values in this field. For regression analysis, the data type of the field must be numeric.
---@field downsample_factor? number Advanced configuration option. Controls the fraction of data that is used to compute the derivatives of the loss function for tree training. A small value results in the use of a small fraction of the data. If this value is set to be less than 1, accuracy typically improves. However, too small a value may result in poor convergence for the ensemble and so require more trees. By default, this value is calculated during hyperparameter optimization. It must be greater than zero and less than or equal to 1.
---@field early_stopping_enabled? boolean Advanced configuration option. Specifies whether the training process should finish if it is not finding any better performing models. If disabled, the training process can take significantly longer and the chance of finding a better performing model is unremarkable.
---@field eta? number Advanced configuration option. The shrinkage applied to the weights. Smaller values result in larger forests which have a better generalization error. However, larger forests cause slower training. By default, this value is calculated during hyperparameter optimization. It must be a value between 0.001 and 1.
---@field eta_growth_rate_per_tree? number Advanced configuration option. Specifies the rate at which `eta` increases for each new tree that is added to the forest. For example, a rate of 1.05 increases `eta` by 5% for each extra tree. By default, this value is calculated during hyperparameter optimization. It must be between 0.5 and 2.
---@field feature_bag_fraction? number Advanced configuration option. Defines the fraction of features that will be used when selecting a random bag for each candidate split. By default, this value is calculated during hyperparameter optimization.
---@field feature_processors? elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisClassificationFeatureProcessorsList Advanced configuration option. A collection of feature preprocessors that modify one or more included fields. The analysis uses the resulting one or more features instead of the original document field. However, these features are ephemeral; they are not stored in the destination index. Multiple `feature_processors` entries can refer to the same document fields. Automatic categorical feature encoding still occurs for the fields that are unprocessed by a custom processor or that have categorical values. Use this property only if you want to override the automatic feature encoding of the specified fields.
---@field gamma? number Advanced configuration option. Regularization parameter to prevent overfitting on the training data set. Multiplies a linear penalty associated with the size of individual trees in the forest. A high gamma value causes training to prefer small trees. A small gamma value results in larger individual trees and slower training. By default, this value is calculated during hyperparameter optimization. It must be a nonnegative value.
---@field lambda? number Advanced configuration option. Regularization parameter to prevent overfitting on the training data set. Multiplies an L2 regularization term which applies to leaf weights of the individual trees in the forest. A high lambda value causes training to favor small leaf weights. This behavior makes the prediction function smoother at the expense of potentially not being able to capture relevant relationships between the features and the dependent variable. A small lambda value results in large individual trees and slower training. By default, this value is calculated during hyperparameter optimization. It must be a nonnegative value.
---@field max_optimization_rounds_per_hyperparameter? number Advanced configuration option. A multiplier responsible for determining the maximum number of hyperparameter optimization steps in the Bayesian optimization procedure. The maximum number of steps is determined based on the number of undefined hyperparameters times the maximum optimization rounds per hyperparameter. By default, this value is calculated during hyperparameter optimization.
---@field max_trees? number Advanced configuration option. Defines the maximum number of decision trees in the forest. The maximum value is 2000. By default, this value is calculated during hyperparameter optimization.
---@field num_top_feature_importance_values? number Advanced configuration option. Specifies the maximum number of feature importance values per document to return. By default, no feature importance calculation occurs.
---@field prediction_field_name? string Defines the name of the prediction field in the results. Defaults to `<dependent_variable>_prediction`.
---@field randomize_seed? number Defines the seed for the random generator that is used to pick training data. By default, it is randomly generated. Set it to a specific value to use the same training data each time you start a job (assuming other related parameters such as `source` and `analyzed_fields` are the same).
---@field soft_tree_depth_limit? number Advanced configuration option. Machine learning uses loss guided tree growing, which means that the decision trees grow where the regularized loss decreases most quickly. This soft limit combines with the `soft_tree_depth_tolerance` to penalize trees that exceed the specified depth; the regularized loss increases quickly beyond this depth. By default, this value is calculated during hyperparameter optimization. It must be greater than or equal to 0.
---@field soft_tree_depth_tolerance? number Advanced configuration option. This option controls how quickly the regularized loss increases when the tree depth exceeds `soft_tree_depth_limit`. By default, this value is calculated during hyperparameter optimization. It must be greater than or equal to 0.01.
---@field training_percent? elasticsearch.TypesPercentage Defines what percentage of the eligible documents that will be used for training. Documents that are ignored by the analysis (for example those that contain arrays with more than one value) won’t be included in the calculation for used percentage.
---@field class_assignment_objective? string
---@field num_top_classes? number Defines the number of categories for which the predicted probabilities are reported. It must be non-negative or -1. If it is -1 or greater than the total number of categories, probabilities are reported for all categories; if you have a large number of categories, there could be a significant effect on the size of your destination index. NOTE: To use the AUC ROC evaluation method, `num_top_classes` must be set to -1 or a value greater than or equal to the total number of categories.

---Advanced configuration option. A collection of feature preprocessors that modify one or more included fields. The analysis uses the resulting one or more features instead of the original document field. However, these features are ephemeral; they are not stored in the destination index. Multiple `feature_processors` entries can refer to the same document fields. Automatic categorical feature encoding still occurs for the fields that are unprocessed by a custom processor or that have categorical values. Use this property only if you want to override the automatic feature encoding of the specified fields.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisClassificationFeatureProcessorsList elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessor[]

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisContainer
---@field classification? elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisClassification The configuration information necessary to perform classification.
---@field outlier_detection? elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisOutlierDetection The configuration information necessary to perform outlier detection. NOTE: Advanced parameters are for fine-tuning classification analysis. They are set automatically by hyperparameter optimization to give the minimum validation error. It is highly recommended to use the default values unless you fully understand the function of these parameters.
---@field regression? elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisRegression The configuration information necessary to perform regression. NOTE: Advanced parameters are for fine-tuning regression analysis. They are set automatically by hyperparameter optimization to give the minimum validation error. It is highly recommended to use the default values unless you fully understand the function of these parameters.

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessor
---@field frequency_encoding? elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorFrequencyEncoding The configuration information necessary to perform frequency encoding.
---@field multi_encoding? elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorMultiEncoding The configuration information necessary to perform multi encoding. It allows multiple processors to be changed together. This way the output of a processor can then be passed to another as an input.
---@field n_gram_encoding? elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorNGramEncoding The configuration information necessary to perform n-gram encoding. Features created by this encoder have the following name format: <feature_prefix>.<ngram><string position>. For example, if the feature_prefix is f, the feature name for the second unigram in a string is f.11.
---@field one_hot_encoding? elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorOneHotEncoding The configuration information necessary to perform one hot encoding.
---@field target_mean_encoding? elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorTargetMeanEncoding The configuration information necessary to perform target mean encoding.

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorFrequencyEncoding
---@field feature_name string The resulting feature name.
---@field field string
---@field frequency_map elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorFrequencyEncodingFrequencyMapMap The resulting frequency map for the field value. If the field value is missing from the frequency_map, the resulting value is 0.

---The resulting frequency map for the field value. If the field value is missing from the frequency_map, the resulting value is 0.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorFrequencyEncodingFrequencyMapMap { [string]: number }

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorMultiEncoding
---@field processors elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorMultiEncodingProcessorsList The ordered array of custom processors to execute. Must be more than 1.

---The ordered array of custom processors to execute. Must be more than 1.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorMultiEncodingProcessorsList number[]

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorNGramEncoding
---@field feature_prefix? string The feature name prefix. Defaults to ngram_<start>_<length>.
---@field field string The name of the text field to encode.
---@field length? number Specifies the length of the n-gram substring. Defaults to 50. Must be greater than 0.
---@field n_grams elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorNGramEncodingNGramsList Specifies which n-grams to gather. It’s an array of integer values where the minimum value is 1, and a maximum value is 5.
---@field start? number Specifies the zero-indexed start of the n-gram substring. Negative values are allowed for encoding n-grams of string suffixes. Defaults to 0.
---@field custom? boolean

---Specifies which n-grams to gather. It’s an array of integer values where the minimum value is 1, and a maximum value is 5.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorNGramEncodingNGramsList number[]

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorOneHotEncoding
---@field field string The name of the field to encode.
---@field hot_map string The one hot map mapping the field value with the column name.

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorTargetMeanEncoding
---@field default_value number The default value if field value is not found in the target_map.
---@field feature_name string The resulting feature name.
---@field field string The name of the field to encode.
---@field target_map elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorTargetMeanEncodingTargetMapMap The field value to target mean transition map.

---The field value to target mean transition map.
---@alias elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisFeatureProcessorTargetMeanEncodingTargetMapMap { [string]: any }

---@class elasticsearch.MlTypesDataframeAnalysisOutlierDetection
---@field compute_feature_influence? boolean Specifies whether the feature influence calculation is enabled.
---@field feature_influence_threshold? number The minimum outlier score that a document needs to have in order to calculate its feature influence score. Value range: 0-1.
---@field method? string The method that outlier detection uses. Available methods are `lof`, `ldof`, `distance_kth_nn`, `distance_knn`, and `ensemble`. The default value is ensemble, which means that outlier detection uses an ensemble of different methods and normalises and combines their individual outlier scores to obtain the overall outlier score.
---@field n_neighbors? number Defines the value for how many nearest neighbors each method of outlier detection uses to calculate its outlier score. When the value is not set, different values are used for different ensemble members. This default behavior helps improve the diversity in the ensemble; only override it if you are confident that the value you choose is appropriate for the data set.
---@field outlier_fraction? number The proportion of the data set that is assumed to be outlying prior to outlier detection. For example, 0.05 means it is assumed that 5% of values are real outliers and 95% are inliers.
---@field standardization_enabled? boolean If true, the following operation is performed on the columns before computing outlier scores: `(x_i - mean(x_i)) / sd(x_i)`.

