---@class elasticsearch.SnapshotTypesAzureRepository
---@field uuid? string
---@field type elasticsearch.SnapshotTypesAzureRepositoryType|string The Azure repository type.
---@field settings? elasticsearch.SnapshotTypesAzureRepositorySettings The repository settings.

---@class elasticsearch.SnapshotTypesAzureRepositorySettings
---@field chunk_size? elasticsearch.TypesByteSize Big files can be broken down into multiple smaller blobs in the blob store during snapshotting. It is not recommended to change this value from its default unless there is an explicit reason for limiting the size of blobs in the repository. Setting a value lower than the default can result in an increased number of API calls to the blob store during snapshot create and restore operations compared to using the default value and thus make both operations slower and more costly. Specify the chunk size as a byte unit, for example: `10MB`, `5KB`, 500B. The default varies by repository type.
---@field compress? boolean When set to `true`, metadata files are stored in compressed format. This setting doesn't affect index files that are already compressed by default.
---@field max_restore_bytes_per_sec? elasticsearch.TypesByteSize The maximum snapshot restore rate per node. It defaults to unlimited. Note that restores are also throttled through recovery settings.
---@field max_snapshot_bytes_per_sec? elasticsearch.TypesByteSize The maximum snapshot creation rate per node. It defaults to 40mb per second. Note that if the recovery settings for managed services are set, then it defaults to unlimited, and the rate is additionally throttled through recovery settings.
---@field base_path? string The path to the repository data within the container. It defaults to the root directory. NOTE: Don't set `base_path` when configuring a snapshot repository for Elastic Cloud Enterprise. Elastic Cloud Enterprise automatically generates the `base_path` for each deployment so that multiple deployments can share the same bucket.
---@field client? string The name of the Azure repository client to use.
---@field container? string The Azure container.
---@field data_access_tier? string The Azure access tier applied to the data blobs of the repository, which hold the files that make up each snapshotted shard. Accepted values are `hot`, `cool` and `cold`, in any letter case. If not set, no access tier is requested and Azure applies the default tier of the storage account.
---@field delete_objects_max_size? number The maxmimum batch size, between 1 and 256, used for `BlobBatch` requests. Defaults to 256 which is the maximum number supported by the Azure blob batch API.
---@field location_mode? string Either `primary_only` or `secondary_only`. Note that if you set it to `secondary_only`, it will force `readonly` to `true`.
---@field max_concurrent_batch_deletes? number The maximum number of concurrent batch delete requests that will be submitted for any individual bulk delete with `BlobBatch`. Note that the effective number of concurrent deletes is further limited by the Azure client connection and event loop thread limits. Defaults to 10, minimum is 1, maximum is 100.
---@field metadata_access_tier? string The Azure access tier applied to the metadata blobs of the repository. These are generally smaller than the data blobs but can be read more often, for example to list the repository contents or to take snapshots. Accepted values are `hot`, `cool` and `cold`, in any letter case. If not set, no access tier is requested and Azure applies the default tier of the storage account.
---@field readonly? boolean If `true`, the repository is read-only. The cluster can retrieve and restore snapshots from the repository but not write to the repository or create snapshots in it. Only a cluster with write access can create snapshots in the repository. All other clusters connected to the repository should have the `readonly` parameter set to `true`. If `false`, the cluster can write to the repository and create snapshots in it. IMPORTANT: If you register the same snapshot repository with multiple clusters, only one cluster should have write access to the repository. Having multiple clusters write to the repository at the same time risks corrupting the contents of the repository.

---The Azure repository type.
---@alias elasticsearch.SnapshotTypesAzureRepositoryType "azure"

