---@class elasticsearch.TransformTypesRetentionPolicy
---@field field string The date field that is used to calculate the age of the document.
---@field max_age elasticsearch.TypesDuration Specifies the maximum age of a document in the destination index. Documents that are older than the configured value are removed from the destination index.

---@class elasticsearch.TransformTypesRetentionPolicyContainer
---@field time? elasticsearch.TransformTypesRetentionPolicy Specifies that the transform uses a time field to set the retention policy.

---The source of the data for the transform.
---@class elasticsearch.TransformTypesSettings
---@field align_checkpoints? boolean Specifies whether the transform checkpoint ranges should be optimized for performance. Such optimization can align checkpoint ranges with the date histogram interval when date histogram is specified as a group source in the transform config. As a result, less document updates in the destination index will be performed thus improving overall performance.
---@field dates_as_epoch_millis? boolean Defines if dates in the output should be written as ISO formatted string or as millis since epoch. epoch_millis was the default for transforms created before version 7.11. For compatible output set this value to `true`.
---@field deduce_mappings? boolean Specifies whether the transform should deduce the destination index mappings from the transform configuration.
---@field docs_per_second? number Specifies a limit on the number of input documents per second. This setting throttles the transform by adding a wait time between search requests. The default value is null, which disables throttling.
---@field max_page_search_size? number Defines the initial page size to use for the composite aggregation for each checkpoint. If circuit breaker exceptions occur, the page size is dynamically adjusted to a lower value. The minimum value is `10` and the maximum is `65,536`. The default value is `500` for `pivot` transforms and `5000` for `latest` transforms.
---@field use_point_in_time? boolean Specifies whether the transform checkpoint will use the Point In Time API while searching over the source index. In general, Point In Time is an optimization that will reduce pressure on the source index by reducing the amount of refreshes and merges, but it can be expensive if a large number of Point In Times are opened and closed for a given index. The benefits and impact depend on the data being searched, the ingest rate into the source index, and the amount of other consumers searching the same source index.
---@field num_failure_retries? number Defines the number of retries on a recoverable failure before the transform task is marked as `failed`. The minimum value is `0` and the maximum is `100`, where `-1` indicates that the transform retries indefinitely. If unset, the cluster-level setting `num_transform_failure_retries` is used. This setting cannot be specified when `unattended` is `true`, because unattended transforms always retry indefinitely.
---@field unattended? boolean If `true`, the transform runs in unattended mode. In unattended mode, the transform retries indefinitely in case of an error which means the transform never fails. Setting the number of retries other than infinite fails in validation.
---@field indexer_request_timeout? elasticsearch.TransformTypesSettingsIndexerRequestTimeout The initial timeout for the requests the transform sends to nodes to retrieve checkpoint information. Each consecutive failure doubles the timeout, up to a maximum of `12h`. The minimum value is `1ms` and the maximum is `12h`. Defaults to `30s`.

---The initial timeout for the requests the transform sends to nodes to retrieve checkpoint information.
---Each consecutive failure doubles the timeout, up to a maximum of `12h`. The minimum value is `1ms` and the
---maximum is `12h`. Defaults to `30s`.
---@alias elasticsearch.TransformTypesSettingsIndexerRequestTimeout elasticsearch.TypesDuration|string

---@class elasticsearch.TransformTypesSource
---@field index elasticsearch.TypesIndices The source indices for the transform. It can be a single index, an index pattern (for example, `"my-index-*""`), an array of indices (for example, `["my-index-000001", "my-index-000002"]`), or an array of index patterns (for example, `["my-index-*", "my-other-index-*"]`. For remote indices use the syntax `"remote_name:index_name"`. If any indices are in remote clusters then the master node and at least one transform node must have the `remote_cluster_client` node role.
---@field query? elasticsearch.TypesQueryDslQueryContainer A query clause that retrieves a subset of data from the source index.
---@field runtime_mappings? elasticsearch.TypesMappingRuntimeFields Definitions of search-time runtime fields that can be used by the transform. For search runtime fields all data nodes, including remote nodes, must be 7.12 or later.

---@class elasticsearch.TransformTypesSyncContainer
---@field time? elasticsearch.TransformTypesTimeSync Specifies that the transform uses a time field to synchronize the source and destination indices.

---@class elasticsearch.TransformTypesTimeSync
---@field delay? elasticsearch.TypesDuration The time delay between the current time and the latest input data time.
---@field field string The date field that is used to identify new documents in the source. In general, it’s a good idea to use a field that contains the ingest timestamp. If you use a different field, you might need to set the delay such that it accounts for data transmission delays.

---@class elasticsearch.TransformUpgradeTransformsRequest
---@field dry_run? boolean When true, the request checks for updates but does not run them.
---@field timeout? elasticsearch.TypesDuration Period to wait for a response. If no response is received before the timeout expires, the request fails and returns an error.

---@class elasticsearch.TransformUpgradeTransformsResponse
---@field needs_update number The number of transforms that need to be upgraded.
---@field no_action number The number of transforms that don’t require upgrading.
---@field updated number The number of transforms that have been upgraded.

