---@class elasticsearch.TypesAnalysisWordDelimiterGraphTokenFilter
---@field version? string
---@field catenate_all? boolean If `true`, the filter produces catenated tokens for chains of alphanumeric characters separated by non-alphabetic delimiters. Defaults to `false`.
---@field catenate_numbers? boolean If `true`, the filter produces catenated tokens for chains of numeric characters separated by non-alphabetic delimiters. Defaults to `false`.
---@field catenate_words? boolean If `true`, the filter produces catenated tokens for chains of alphabetical characters separated by non-alphabetic delimiters. Defaults to `false`.
---@field generate_number_parts? boolean If `true`, the filter includes tokens consisting of only numeric characters in the output. If `false`, the filter excludes these tokens from the output. Defaults to `true`.
---@field generate_word_parts? boolean If `true`, the filter includes tokens consisting of only alphabetical characters in the output. If `false`, the filter excludes these tokens from the output. Defaults to `true`.
---@field preserve_original? elasticsearch.SpecUtilsStringifiedboolean If `true`, the filter includes the original version of any split tokens in the output. This original version includes non-alphanumeric delimiters. Defaults to `false`.
---@field protected_words? elasticsearch.TypesAnalysisWordDelimiterGraphTokenFilterProtectedWordsList Array of tokens the filter won’t split.
---@field protected_words_path? string Path to a file that contains a list of tokens the filter won’t split. This path must be absolute or relative to the `config` location, and the file must be UTF-8 encoded. Each token in the file must be separated by a line break.
---@field split_on_case_change? boolean If `true`, the filter splits tokens at letter case transitions. For example: camelCase -> [ camel, Case ]. Defaults to `true`.
---@field split_on_numerics? boolean If `true`, the filter splits tokens at letter-number transitions. For example: j2se -> [ j, 2, se ]. Defaults to `true`.
---@field stem_english_possessive? boolean If `true`, the filter removes the English possessive (`'s`) from the end of each token. For example: O'Neil's -> [ O, Neil ]. Defaults to `true`.
---@field type_table? elasticsearch.TypesAnalysisWordDelimiterGraphTokenFilterTypeTableList Array of custom type mappings for characters. This allows you to map non-alphanumeric characters as numeric or alphanumeric to avoid splitting on those characters.
---@field type_table_path? string Path to a file that contains custom type mappings for characters. This allows you to map non-alphanumeric characters as numeric or alphanumeric to avoid splitting on those characters.
---@field type elasticsearch.TypesAnalysisWordDelimiterGraphTokenFilterType|string
---@field adjust_offsets? boolean If `true`, the filter adjusts the offsets of split or catenated tokens to better reflect their actual position in the token stream. Defaults to `true`.
---@field ignore_keywords? boolean If `true`, the filter skips tokens with a keyword attribute of true. Defaults to `false`.

---Array of tokens the filter won’t split.
---@alias elasticsearch.TypesAnalysisWordDelimiterGraphTokenFilterProtectedWordsList string[]

