---@class elasticsearch.UpdateByQueryRequest
---@field index string A comma-separated list of data streams, indices, and aliases to search. It supports wildcards (`*`). To search all data streams or indices, omit this parameter or use `*` or `_all`.
---@field allow_no_indices? boolean A setting that does two separate checks on the index expression. If `false`, the request returns an error (1) if any wildcard expression (including `_all` and `*`) resolves to zero matching indices or (2) if the complete set of resolved indices, aliases or data streams is empty after all expressions are evaluated. If `true`, index expressions that resolve to no indices are allowed and the request returns an empty result.
---@field analyzer? string The analyzer to use for the query string. This parameter can be used only when the `q` query string parameter is specified.
---@field analyze_wildcard? boolean If `true`, wildcard and prefix queries are analyzed. This parameter can be used only when the `q` query string parameter is specified.
---@field conflicts? elasticsearch.TypesConflicts|string The preferred behavior when update by query hits version conflicts: `abort` or `proceed`.
---@field default_operator? elasticsearch.TypesQueryDslOperator|string The default operator for query string query: `and` or `or`. This parameter can be used only when the `q` query string parameter is specified.
---@field df? string The field to use as default where no field prefix is given in the query string. This parameter can be used only when the `q` query string parameter is specified.
---@field expand_wildcards? elasticsearch.TypesExpandWildcards The type of index that wildcard patterns can match. If the request can target data streams, this argument determines whether wildcard expressions match hidden data streams. It supports comma-separated values, such as `open,hidden`.
---@field from? number Skips the specified number of documents.
---@field ignore_unavailable? boolean If `false`, the request returns an error if it targets a concrete (non-wildcarded) index, alias, or data stream that is missing, closed, or otherwise unavailable. If `true`, unavailable concrete targets are silently ignored.
---@field lenient? boolean If `true`, format-based query failures (such as providing text to a numeric field) in the query string will be ignored. This parameter can be used only when the `q` query string parameter is specified.
---@field max_docs? number The maximum number of documents to process. It defaults to all documents. When set to a value less than or equal to `scroll_size` and `conflicts` is set to `abort`, a scroll will not be used to retrieve the results for the operation.
---@field pipeline? string The ID of the pipeline to use to preprocess incoming documents. If the index has a default ingest pipeline specified, then setting the value to `_none` disables the default ingest pipeline for this request. If a final pipeline is configured it will always run, regardless of the value of this parameter.
---@field preference? string The node or shard the operation should be performed on. It is random by default.
---@field q? string A query in the Lucene query string syntax.
---@field refresh? boolean If `true`, Elasticsearch refreshes affected shards to make the operation visible to search after the request completes. This is different than the update API's `refresh` parameter, which causes just the shard that received the request to be refreshed.
---@field request_cache? boolean If `true`, the request cache is used for this request. It defaults to the index-level setting.
---@field requests_per_second? number The maximum number of documents to update per second, across the entire update_by_query operation (including slices). It can be either `-1` to turn off throttling or any decimal number like `1.7` or `12` to throttle to that level.
---@field routing? elasticsearch.TypesRouting A custom value used to route operations to a specific shard. Not allowed when `index.slice.enabled` is `true` for the target index; use `_slice` instead.
---@field scroll? elasticsearch.TypesDuration The period to retain the search context for scrolling.
---@field scroll_size? number The size of the scroll request that powers the operation.
---@field search_timeout? elasticsearch.TypesDuration An explicit timeout for each search request. By default, there is no timeout.
---@field search_type? elasticsearch.TypesSearchType|string The type of the search operation. Available options include `query_then_fetch` and `dfs_query_then_fetch`.
---@field slices? elasticsearch.TypesSlices The number of slices this task should be divided into.
---@field sort? elasticsearch.UpdateByQueryRequestSortList A comma-separated list of <field>:<direction> pairs.
---@field stats? elasticsearch.UpdateByQueryRequestStatsList The specific `tag` of the request for logging and statistical purposes.
---@field terminate_after? number The maximum number of documents to collect for each shard. If a query reaches this limit, Elasticsearch terminates the query early. Elasticsearch collects documents before sorting. IMPORTANT: Use with caution. Elasticsearch applies this parameter to each shard handling the request. When possible, let Elasticsearch perform early termination automatically. Avoid specifying this parameter for requests that target data streams with backing indices across multiple data tiers.
---@field timeout? elasticsearch.TypesDuration The period each update request waits for the following operations: dynamic mapping updates, waiting for active shards. By default, it is one minute. This guarantees Elasticsearch waits for at least the timeout before failing. The actual wait time could be longer, particularly when multiple waits occur.
---@field version? boolean If `true`, returns the document version as part of a hit.
---@field version_type? boolean Should the document increment the version number (internal) on hit or not (reindex)
---@field wait_for_active_shards? elasticsearch.TypesWaitForActiveShards The number of shard copies that must be active before proceeding with the operation. Set to `all` or any positive integer up to the total number of shards in the index (`number_of_replicas+1`). The `timeout` parameter controls how long each write request waits for unavailable shards to become available. Both work exactly the way they work in the bulk API. Update by query uses scrolled searches, so you can also specify the `scroll` parameter to control how long it keeps the search context alive, for example `?scroll=10m`.
---@field wait_for_completion? boolean If `true`, the request blocks until the operation is complete. If `false`, Elasticsearch performs some preflight checks, launches the request, and returns a task ID that you can use to cancel or get the status of the task. Elasticsearch creates a record of this task as a document at `.tasks/task/${taskId}`.
---@field query? elasticsearch.TypesQueryDslQueryContainer The documents to update using the Query DSL.
---@field runtime_mappings? elasticsearch.TypesMappingRuntimeFields One or more runtime fields in the search request. These fields take precedence over mapped fields with the same name.
---@field script? elasticsearch.TypesScript The script to run to update the document source or metadata when updating.
---@field slice? elasticsearch.TypesSlicedScroll Slice the request manually using the provided slice ID and total number of slices.

---@alias elasticsearch.UpdateByQueryRequestSortList string[]

---@alias elasticsearch.UpdateByQueryRequestStatsList string[]

---@class elasticsearch.UpdateByQueryResponse
---@field batches? number The number of scroll responses pulled back by the update by query.
---@field failures? elasticsearch.UpdateByQueryResponseFailuresList Array of failures if there were any unrecoverable errors during the process. If this is non-empty then the request ended because of those failures. Update by query is implemented using batches. Any failure causes the entire process to end, but all failures in the current batch are collected into the array. You can use the `conflicts` option to prevent reindex from ending when version conflicts occur.
---@field noops? number The number of documents that were ignored because the script used for the update by query returned a noop value for `ctx.op`.
---@field deleted? number The number of documents that were successfully deleted.
---@field requests_per_second? number The number of requests per second effectively run during the update by query.
---@field retries? elasticsearch.TypesRetries The number of retries attempted by update by query. `bulk` is the number of bulk actions retried. `search` is the number of search actions retried.
---@field slices? elasticsearch.UpdateByQueryResponseSlicesList Status of each slice if the update by query was sliced
---@field task? string
---@field timed_out? boolean If true, some requests timed out during the update by query.
---@field took? number The number of milliseconds from start to end of the whole operation.
---@field total? number The number of documents that were successfully processed.
---@field updated? number The number of documents that were successfully updated.
---@field version_conflicts? number The number of version conflicts that the update by query hit.
---@field throttled? elasticsearch.TypesDuration
---@field throttled_millis? number The number of milliseconds the request slept to conform to `requests_per_second`.
---@field throttled_until? elasticsearch.TypesDuration
---@field throttled_until_millis? number This field should always be equal to zero in an _update_by_query response. It only has meaning when using the task API, where it indicates the next time (in milliseconds since epoch) a throttled request will be run again in order to conform to `requests_per_second`.

